  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 221 AM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nMorning Fog & Storm Chances Staying West Today:\n\nIt'll be a foggy start to the day with light winds and stout surface \nmoisture allowing for widespread patchy fog development across the \nregion. To our west, across Western and Central Minnesota, showers \nand thunderstorms are expected to develop late tonight into the \nmorning hours along the leading edge of some theta-e advection. \nAdditional convection is expected to develop through the afternoon \nalong an axis of instability just to our west. A stout layer of warm \nand dry air in the low levels across our area around 850 mb should \nkeep areas east of the I-35 corridor capped enough to limit any \nconvective development making its way into our area. \n\nIncreasing Rain Chances Through Start Of New Week:\n\nBy Monday, an upper wave will begin to eject out of the Pacific \nNorthwest and into the Northern High Plains. At the surface, a low \npressure center is expected to move out of the Northern Rockies and \ninto the Canadian Plains, gradually occluding into midweek. As the \nupper level trough axis moves into the region, it will encroach on a \nvery moist warm sector and provide some additional support aloft, \nsetting off convection. 05.18Z ensemble soundings do not show a \nparticularly unstable environment across our area with a strong cap \nin the low levels noted but pWats could very likely be in the 1.75-\n2+ inch range so heavy rainfall along and ahead of the frontal \nboundary will be something to keep an eye on. Storm motion and \noverall boundary movement look to be progressive enough to limit any \nsignificant flash flooding concerns but 0.5-1 inch totals over a 6-\n12 hour period are not out of the realm of possibilities. The \nheaviest rain should occur through the day on Tuesday with chances \ndecreasing through the overnight hours as moisture gets scoured out \nby the frontal boundary early Wednesday morning. \n\nWednesday - Next Weekend: Drying Out, Near Normal Temperatures \n\nAs the upper wave/trough axis move east, the trailing frontal \nboundary from the occluding surface low well to our north in Canada \nwill sweep through the region. This will finally usher in a drier \nairmass to the region as a whole with dew points falling back into \nthe upper 50s. A weak surface high is expected to build into the \nMidwest by Thursday. This, in combination with zonal flow to weak \nridging aloft, will keep conditions dry for the late week period. \nTemperatures behind the front won't be much \"cooler\" but they will \nbe a bit closer to normal in the 70s to lower 80s Thursday and \nFriday. The next chances for rain look to come by the weekend as \nanother upper wave looks to potentially swing through the upper \nMidwest.",
